B+ Tree fixes both:
 it stores all actual data only in leaf nodes, and internal nodes hold only guide keys (copies, used purely for navigation). 
 On top of that, all leaf nodes are linked together in a sorted chain — turning range queries into a simple linked-list walk. This is exactly why every real database index (MySQL InnoDB, PostgreSQL, Oracle) and filesystem (NTFS, ext4) uses B+ trees, not plain B-trees.

Structure

A B+ tree of order m has two kinds of nodes, and they follow different rules:

Internal (index) nodes
 - Hold only keys, used purely to guide the search — no actual data attached.
 - Max keys = m - 1, max children = m (same as B-tree).
 - Min children (non-root) = ⌈m/2⌉.
 - A key appearing in an internal node is just a copy — the real data still lives in a leaf.


Leaf nodes
 - Hold all the actual keys/data — every single key inserted into the tree ends up in some leaf.
 - Max keys = m - 1 (sometimes trees use a different leaf capacity L, but for simplicity we use the same m).
 - Every leaf node has a pointer to the next leaf node (next), forming a sorted singly-linked list across  
   the bottom of the tree.
 - Leaves are all at the same depth — same balance guarantee as a B-tree.


Picture of the shape:

                    [   internal nodes — pure signposts   ]
                   /              |                \
              [ internal / leaf pointers guide search down ]
             /        |          |          \
        [leaf] --> [leaf] --> [leaf] --> [leaf] --> NULL
         (all actual data, linked left to right)
The core rule that makes it a B+ tree (not a B-tree)

Every key that appears in an internal node must also appear in a leaf. Internal nodes never "hide" data — they only borrow a copy of a key to use as a signpost.

This single rule is what separates B+ from B-tree, and it's why searching, splitting, and traversing all behave differently.